course: dictionary
lesson: 1868
title: A colander and its job
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A colander is a kitchen bowl with lots of small holes. You use it to drain water from food, such as pasta, or to wash vegetables.
explain: «Draining pasta is a separation: the holes of the colander are big enough for water but too small for the pasta.» The water runs out through the holes, and the pasta stays inside. Ask yourself: what gets through, and what stays behind?
explain: Look at the words around colander. «Drain in a colander» and «rinse in a colander» both tell you it is used with water. Safety first: an adult should pour boiling water into the colander.
fact: A colander is a kitchen bowl with lots of small holes.
fact: «A colander or a filter lets small things through and keeps bigger things back.»
fact: A tea bag is a filter too, because the tea gets through but the leaves stay back.
fact: A colander separates a solid, like pasta, from a liquid, like water.
fact: An adult should always pour boiling water into a colander.
exercise: What is a colander? | A tall glass jug | A spoon for stirring soup | *A kitchen bowl full of small holes | A pair of kitchen scissors
exercise: When you drain pasta in a colander, what gets through the holes? | The colander itself | The pasta | The whole saucepan | *Water
exercise: Who should pour boiling water into a colander? | *An adult | A child on their own | A visitor who is not looking | The family dog
test: «A colander or a filter lets small things through and keeps bigger things back.» What does a colander keep back? | Water only | *Bigger things | Small things | Nothing at all
test: Which of these is also a filter, like a colander? | A pair of shoes | A frying pan | *A tea bag | A window
exam: Which sentence uses colander correctly? | *Rinse the vegetables in a colander | Colander, please, the pasta is hot | Colander the vegetables and eat them | A colander is a kind of bread
